'''''Dioon spinulosum''''', '''giant dioon''' or '''spiny dioon''', is a [[cycad]] endemic to limestone cliffs and rocky hillsides in the [[tropical rainforest]]s of [[Veracruz]] and [[Oaxaca]], Mexico. It is one of the tallest cycads in the world, growing to 16&nbsp;meters in height. The tree is found at low elevations to 300&nbsp;meters above sea level.

''Dioon spinulosum'' prefers well-drained soil with regular water. It will grow in soils containing few nutrients, in soils rich in limestone, and on slopes.

==Description==
[[File:Dioon spinulosum, El Paraíso Zacatal, Oaxaca, Mexico 179029267.jpg|left|thumb|Mature ''Dioon spinulosum'', Oaxaca, Mexico]]
''Dioon spinulosum'' may exceptionally reach as much as {{convert|16|m|sp=us}} in height, but is more typically {{cvt|3–6|m|0}} tall.{{sfn|Norstog|Nicholls|1997|p=14}} Mature leaves are numerous and measure {{cvt|1.5–2|m|ft}} with 140 to 240 leaflets.{{sfn|Jones|1998|p=174}} The seed cones of the species are among the largest of the cycads.{{sfn|Norstog|Nicholls|1997|p=14}} They measure almost {{convert|80|cm|sp=us}} in length together with its stem and {{cvt|30|cm}} in diameter.{{sfn|Norstog|Nicholls|1997|p=278}}

==Taxonomy==
''Dioon spinulosum'' was scientifically described and named by [[August Wilhelm Eichler]] crediting [[William Turner Thiselton-Dyer]] in 1883. It is classified in the genus ''[[Dioon]]'' as part of the family [[Zamiaceae]].{{sfn|Hassler|2025}} According to [[Plants of the World Online]] it has no [[subspecies]] or [[Synonym (botany)|synonyms]].{{sfn|POWO 2025}}

===Names===
The species name, ''spinulosum'', means "with small spines" referring to the edges of the leaves.{{sfn|Jones|1998|p=174}} It is known by the [[common name]]s ''giant dioon'',{{sfn|IFAS Extension|2025}} ''spiny dioon'',{{sfn|Broschat|Meerow|1991|p=103}} and ''gum palm''.{{sfn|Govan|Kahn|2005|p=11}} In Spanish it is known as {{lang|es|Coyolito de Cerro}}.{{sfn|IUCN 2022}}

==Range and habitat==
''Dioon spinulosum'' is found wild in the Mexican states of Oaxaca and Veracruz in a total of three locations.{{sfn|IUCN 2022}} It was more widely distributed in the past, but now grows in the lowlands of the [[Sierra Madre Oriental]] mountains.{{sfn|Jones|1998|p=174}} It grows on limestone hills and cliffs in the tropical evergreen rainforest there. It can be found at altitudes of {{convert|30 to 300|m|-1|sp=us}} above sea level. It is typically a part of the [[understory]] with only a few individuals emerging from the forest canopy.{{sfn|IUCN 2022}}

==Cultivation==
In cultivation it is slow growing, and has a medium tolerance for salt.{{sfn|Broschat|Meerow|1991|p=103}} It is cold hardy in [[USDA zones]] 9B to 11.{{sfn|IFAS Extension|2025}}

